  "findings": [
    "ALLDIFF16:66.1% ordinaryCP and40.7% residual hybrid. Full support pipeline includes value-coordinate conversion,graph construction,matching,SCC and support decoding; matching-only is a much smaller target.",
    "Hybrid16:residual extraction11.7%+encoding2.6%;SUM revision14.4%,split into native call7.5% and Python adapter/application6.9%. Domain/state operations6.4%;other CP propagation7.6%.",
    "Hybrid9:residual call67.4%,ALLDIFF9.5%,SUM4.9%; prioritize native enumeration/pruning there rather than assume the16 profile applies universally.",
    "20prefix:ALLDIFF34.1%,SUM46.3%,of which38.3% is already inside native SUM call. No residual enumeration and no complete solution. Optimizing native DP word ranges could be a subsequent candidate; no implementation claim.",
    "Hybrid14 has sizable search-other/plan/explanation overhead in the single wall pass; avoid overinterpreting small-workload percentages. ALLDIFF remains the largest individual revision category."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Native SUM call timing includes ctypes transition. The Python SUM remainder includes mask projection,cache checks,serialization,decoding and domain application; it is not solely packing overhead.",
    "SUM breakdown is nested inside sum_filtering,not an additional disjoint time category.",
    "Profiling preserves nodes,failures,revisions,solutions,termination,residual counters and native SUM path counters across all five passes.",
    "C internals are opaque to cProfile; neither the residual C algorithm nor C SUM instruction-level hot spots are established by Python profiles.",
    "No new ALLDIFF kernel implemented. Production defaults unchanged."
  ],
  "checks": [
    "Main40passes and20prefix10passes completed with identical search counters and valid complete squares.",
    "Frozen source snapshots and compiled-library cache metadata retained."
  ],
  "reproduce": [
